INT: In terms of your experience as a young child during this time, did you ever daydream or have goals about what you wanted to do or be growing up?

AS: I don't remember having any idea of, you know, having a profession when I was [UNCLEAR] at that stage. And, you know, during the war, the focus was on surviving, and I was very much aware of that. So, you know, thinking of leaving, my parents started learning English, just in case. But, it was, you know, "Next year in Jerusalem..." [LAUGHING] So we expected, actually, to end up in Israel. But it didn't happen, things didn't go that way.

INT: And so, this notion, this idea of having to leave, would you say that that was felt for you from a very young age?

AS: Yes, yes. The idea of not belonging, the idea of impermanence, the idea of maybe, someday, we can have a home where there are no anti-Semites. That was the goal.

INT: Did your parents talk to you often about anti-Semitism or try to teach you, you know, talk to you at home about what was happening in your community at that time? Or they just wanted you to be a normal-- You know, have a happy childhood?

AS: I'm sure they wanted me to have a happy childhood, but it was so pervasive that you didn't even have to say it. And I remember when my mother had to sew the yellow stars on the coats and on the jackets, and she was so mad.

INT: Did she say anything that you recall when she was mad? Did she--

AS: So, again, I don't remember if she said anything, but I remember the feel of that day when she was mad and my father was, sort of, "Okay, we have to do this. We're going to do it."

INT: Do you remember how old you were when that happened?

AS: I must have been very young. I don't remember when it happened, but it must have been at the beginning.

INT: Do you know who it was that told Jews in your community that the stars had to be on clothing? Was it the Romanian authorities?

AS: The authorities were Romanian authorities in that part of Transylvania. The northern part of Transylvania belonged to Hungary. The rest of it belonged to Romania.

So these were Romanian authorities.

INT: Okay. Okay.

AS: The thing with the Romanian authorities during the war and forever, really, since the Turkish occupation in 1400, the whole country functions... I shouldn't say, "functions" but it does, "functioned," let's put it this way. The country functioned based on bribes and arranging things. So it was all, you know, a matter of, that was the synagogue, I think that, people went to the synagogue to put their heads together to strategize. "Who are they going to bribe? How are they going to go about this or that? What's happening?" So, the synagogue was the center of the Jewish life.

And, at some point, Jews were not allowed to have businesses, which meant that my father was not allowed to have his lumberyard anymore. So, that would have left us in pretty bad shape. So his idea was to sell the lumberyard to somebody, on paper. And there was a very nice young man in town who was an alcoholic. He was terrible, it was terrible for the family. And, they couldn't even afford to live separately for their in-laws. So, they lived together. And so my father offered this guy the lumberyard, put it on his name, and the guy had not-- He didn't have to do anything at all. My father would pay him every month or every week or whenever he would go to their house-- Actually, I went with him once to their house to give the money to the wife, because, otherwise, he would drink it all. And, that's how we survived. Now, of course, nobody was crazy enough to believe this story, so the authorities had to be bribed, and, they were. I remember my father inviting the chief of police, the mayor, all these people to a game of poker. And my mother, for some reason, I remember my mother was making donuts and they were eating donuts and were very happy. And my father kept losing and losing, and losing and laughing, because he was, actually, a very good poker player, but he deliberately lost enough for these people to, whatever, have their bribes. And that's how things went on.

INT: And, so, did he end up selling the lumberyard...

AS: On paper.

INT: ...to this person?

INT: On paper?

AS: Yes. Yes.

INT: And, after that happened, did you and your parents continue to live in this area, or did you have to...

AS: So, after that, the sergeant's family moved in, the story that I told before, and we moved out of the area and we lived not for very long, maybe a year and something in this beautiful place.

INT: The mashed potatoes on the silver platter.

INT: [LAUGHING]

AS: [LAUGHING]

INT: And after you lived at this nice home, then where did you go?

AS: So after we lived in this nice home, it came... The war was coming to an end, and my father said that it wasn't safe for us to stay in Transylvania, that all the armies, the Russians going back home, the Germans being taken prisoner, the Italians coming from Greece, all these armies and the Romanians would cross exactly where we were, and we had to go somewhere else. [PAUSES 6 SECONDS] [GESTURING]

So, we had to go somewhere else. And, so, we did move, we went to Bucharest, and that's a longer story of how we got there. [GESTURING]

INT: And when did your family have to move next?

AS: So my father decided that we had to get out of where we were and move south towards Bucharest, to Bucharest, actually, where the war seemed to be over already. Bucharest was bombed during the war, but now the war was over in that area. So, that's the place where we should go. So we packed the suitcase, and put everything in the suitcase that we were going to take, and we took some food and we went to my grandmother's, my mother's mother, and they had also packed and cooked and did the laundry just like us. So, our suitcases, they put them in a cart.

I don't know, [GESTURING] maybe four feet by three feet or something like that. Our suitcases, their suitcases, and, everybody, all the the adults had knapsacks filled with stuff. And the men pulled these-- pulled the cart. And we started walking, and we walked for about a week. The trains were not running, so we had to walk and we started walking. And I have to say, I had a great time because it was interesting. You know, we were in different places. Every night we slept in somebody else's house as we would get to a village where we decided we could stop. We looked for a room, and somebody was always willing to rent a room overnight. And, I thought that was very interesting because I always liked to see how other people lived, and it was funny to see some houses where everything was covered in plastic, and some rooms were open only for guests and special occasions. So, we we went like this for about a week until, we got to a place where the trains were running. On the way there, something very strange happened. As I was looking at the scenery, I noticed a man hanging from a tree, which was a very strange sight. And I said to myself, "The men don't belong into the trees." And at the same time, my mother and my aunt covered, [GESTURING] horrified, covered their eyes, and my father told me to look at some bird in the other direction, which I did, but I knew that there was something I was better off not looking at, so I didn't insist on that. And, on the way there, we also ran into some Romanian soldiers. And I remember my mother saying, "Oh, they're so young." So we stopped and we exchanged a few words and they gave us sardines, and we gave them homemade cake, and it was all very friendly. A little later, we heard an explosion, and it turned out that as they were crossing a bridge, the Germans bombed the bridge, so they all died. That was kind of a sad story. And now, I should have said something about the alarms, I remember now. When we were in the small town where we were living, there were already alarms that sounded air raids, and so everybody had to go to the cellar, hide in the cellar. And there was one sound when the alarm started, and then, when the danger was over, there was a second different sound that you could go back to the house. So for, you know, for most people, for the adults, these were very stressful times. Although, this was a very small town, the only thing they had was a glass factory at the time. So, my mother was always in a panic and my father was always very calm, and he told me that this is a very big place and the bomb is not going to fall exactly here, which I believed and which is what happened. [LAUGHING] I don't think they bombed the town, really, at all, as far as I know. It wasn't worth bombing, but Bucharest had been bombed. So, when we got to Bucharest, after getting on a train at Brașov, this is where the train started running. We got on the train, we got to Bucharest. We saw a lot of houses, a lot of ruins, houses that had been destroyed during the war. So when we got to Bucharest by train, we arrived to the major train station called the North Train Station, which was modeled after the Paris North Train Station, very nice with a glass dome. And, we went to a hotel and we stayed in a hotel temporarily, for a while, not knowing what was going to happen. Then we moved to what was called Pantheon. The Pantheon was a place where you you got a room and you also got meals. And that's when things started going bad as far as the food was concerned, because there was no food. So it was very bad food. I, you know, I decided I wasn't going to eat any bad food, so I lost a lot of weight, but, you know, otherwise, I was very happy. We lived in this Pantheon, it was in the center of the town. There was a park nearby, and my mother for the first time, she had no kitchen to cook in and no house to clean, so she spent her time with me, which, from my point of view, was the best arrangement. And, so, we used to go to the park and we used to go to the movies, and that was also a great thing.

INT: Agnes, do you remember, approximately, how old you were at this time when your family made the journey to Bucharest?

AS: Yes. So, it was 1945, I was seven-years-old, and after living in this Pantheon, we had a room in somebody's house. We were recommended by this lady to another lady who had been the lady-in-waiting to the Queen of Romania. But, also, she had hit hard times and she needed some income, so she rented out this room and it was in a wonderful, beautiful neighborhood. And later on, we actually rented an apartment in that neighborhood, just like a street away from where we had been. And it was a completely different life in Bucharest. It was a big city, it's the capital, and there were streetcars and some cars. My aunt got some kind of an agoraphobia and she was afraid to cross the street. I guess, as a result of, you know, everything else that happened. And, so my grandmother, my aunt and my uncle, who had come with us when we came to Bucharest, went back to the town where they used to live. But we stayed in Bucharest, we never went back. My father sold the business. And, so, he had money to start another business in Bucharest.

And he had heard how all his family died, with one exception, the sister who was a pharmacist. So he was hoping that somehow, sometime, the sister, somewhere the sister was alive and he was going to find her. And he, based on this hope, really, he decided to start another business, which was a pharmaceutical company, a small manufacturing pharmaceutical company. Temporarily, he took a partner who was a pharmacist and the company did very well, very well. But the sister never came back. And, it was time for me to go to school, I was seven-years-old, and I was sent to a French school, where nobody spoke anything but French from day one. So that was a little bit of a challenge. And they had no remedial courses for anybody, I don't know. Some of the children who were there had had governesses, French governesses, so they were at a disadvantage. So, it was a bit of a struggle in the beginning, especially. But, eventually, I learned French the hard way. And, then, what happened, because I had been-- I had such an isolated life as a child, I had not been exposed to any kind of childhood diseases that you get vaccinated for these days. So, my first two years in school, I was sick with everything, one thing after the other. I even had certain, like, scarlet fever, which they said you were supposed to have only once. I had it twice. So I had everything in the world. And, you know, with scarlet fever, once I was hospitalized because it was contagious and there was nothing, there were no antibiotics. So, eventually, I got well after all this. But the good part of being sick so much, there was a good part, not that I missed school, that was not a good part, I wasn't happy about that. But I really started reading, and I started reading from the age eight. I read even novels, I read everything that came into my hands in every language that I could read in. So, that was a very good thing. And, we had a very good life, very good financially, between 1945 and 1948. So, you know, I was rich for three years. Then, that was it. Before that, it wasn't, and after that, not. In 1948, what happened, Romania had a democratic government right after the war. But little by little, the communists, who had some representation from the very beginning, they took over. So we ended up by 1948, there was a communist government. So, what they did, the peasants took the land from the landlords and all industry was nationalized, so my father's business was just taken by the State. One day, they came, the workers came with a paper and they said, "Sign the business over to the people." And he said, "I'm not signing." And they said, "You're not going home." So he was there for a while, and, finally, he had to sign, there was no choice. So he signed the business over, and, that was the end of good times, really. We stayed in the apartment, in the nice apartment where we lived until I left. Until my mother and my brother left, also. But, otherwise, it was very hard. Luckily, my father was such a good accountant that he managed to get a job as the chief accountant. You know, some different jobs as an inspector, then as a chief accountant in a very important, state-owned, civil engineering military outfit.

So, the French school was closed. And three years, and that was it, that was all the education I got there. I was transferred to a state school, a neighboring school, about two minutes away from the house. I came in knowing a lot more than the kids in public school, which was useful, and all the French teachers left, except for one, they were all French. They all went back to France, except for one, who was married to a Romanian man. And this was my luck, she lived on the same street where we lived. And I took French lessons for I don't know how many years. So our financial situation was so bad that we could-- that, sometimes, we couldn't make ends meet. And, you know, mostly we got by. The food was just, you know, mostly potatoes and bread, rarely meat. And, it was tough times.

So my father, after they took the business, my father was taken to prison. You wonder why. Because he was a capitalist, so-called capitalist. And they found some excuse, they took him to prison. He was in prison, and in the Romanian system, during the communists, you were guilty as accused. You did not have to-- They did not have to prove your guilt, you had to prove your innocence. So that's how things worked. And he, somehow or other, I don't know, a lawyer. Finally, after about six weeks of prison, he was let out. Okay, he went back, he went to work. Well, there was a second time when he was in prison, when he was the chief accountant of this important construction company. Somebody stole a set of tires, these big tires that they use on construction machinery. They didn't know who stole them, so they took the entire management, chief accountant, chief engineer, director, chief of human resources, everybody went to prison. And now it was their job to prove that they didn't take anything. And that was very disturbing because they said that he owed something like two million or-- I mean, it was a ridiculous amount. They figured out how much revenue the two tires would have brought. It was completely Kafkaesque situation. So, anyway, somehow or other, they found the guy who stole them, and, so, they let all these other people go. But this was more serious than the first time, he was sent to a prison in a different town. I went to visit him, I was about 15-years-old, I went with the other wives, and he was behind bars, literally behind bars, and he looked very pale.

But my father always said that everything was fine. The first time, "Everything was fine. I'll get out." The second time, "Everything's fine. We didn't do anything. We will get out." You know, the guards were very nice to them. And he, actually, eventually, got out. He was very lucky that he was not a political prisoner who were treated differently, and most of them never even came back.

So, the Communist Romania had all sorts of ideas about what next projects to complete. At some point, they wanted to build a canal between the Danube and the Black Sea, so that's the southern part of Romania. And they had the political prisoners working on digging this canal. And I met somebody who actually worked there, and they worked in so much mud that they couldn't walk. The tunnel was so low, they couldn't stand, so everybody they made these sleds, everybody was on their belly digging, and there were mosquitoes. The job was-- The project was never completed. It was a very bad idea, altogether. This area is, ecologically, a treasure even to this day, now that they left it alone. So they destroyed that for several years. [COUGHING] And... Nothing came of it except that they killed a lot of people. [DRINKING]

INT: The second time, your dad was in prison, how long was he there?

AS: I mean, it seemed like a really long time. It was probably two months or more, or longer.

You know, the prison for him, it wasn't-- I mean, the guards were nice. At some point, somebody asked, "Who can count well?" And others said, "Oh, he can count," pointing to my father who was an accountant. So, they had him clean toilets.

They were nice. And, when they went to court for their case, there were some political prisoners there and they said they didn't have anything to eat for several days. So all these other prisoners had crumbs in their pockets and they all took out the crumbs for these people to eat. It was really unthinkable. Unthinkable. I can say that probably because I was older and much more aware of what was going on, Communism was even worse than, from our point of view, than World War II.

INT: From your memories, what was the attitude of the non-Jewish population towards the Jewish population in Bucharest at that time, under communism?

AS: So, there was always anti-Semitism. Romanians, there were some who were anti-Semites. Most were anti-Semites, perhaps, but I had Romanian friends, and, you know, there was no-- In school, I was with Romanian children. Very few were Jewish. And we were not persecuted, persecuted. However, the official attitude was that, "You know, Jewish people have equal rights to religion and free religion and everything, but we don't like the Zionists. Israel is bad. It's a capitalist country, whatever." So, that was the attitude. A lot of Jews changed their names to make them more Romanian-sounding because there was discrimination. [PAUSES] We never changed our name, even though it was unpronounceable for Romanians. [LAUGHING] The first time I heard my name pronounced right was when we left and we landed, we went to Austria. At the border, when they read my name, it was perfect.

INT: Yeah. And, I know you mentioned you were in this French school until about 1948, and you were probably about ten-years-old when that closed.

AS: Yes.

INT: Did you go to school after that time, like, you said, the local school?

AS: Yes.

INT: Neighborhood school?

AS: Yes.

INT: Okay. And did that go up until high school?

AS: Yes. There were ten years of school in Romania. So, after the 10th grade, you were finished.

INT: Okay. Okay. And so, your teenage years, you were in school. Did you have particular experiences or memories from those teenage years in school that stick out to you?

AS: So, I was a very good student, and Romanian education was good in sciences and math. Otherwise, they were rewriting history, they were rewriting geography. They were rewriting everything. So, you know, they were biased against the previous regime. And, also, it was very limiting. We learned mostly Romanian literature, not mostly, only Romanian literature, Romanian history, geography-- You had to learn other countries, too. And, even the books that were printed, they were only printing Romanian books and Russian literature, which actually was very good, but Soviet literature as well. You couldn't find any foreign books. So what people did was, everybody had a library at home, and we would borrow books from one another. And that's how I managed to read a lot.

INT: Did you have any favorite novels during that time? Like, as a teenager? Any favorite books? [DRINKING]

So I don't know if my taste was the same as it is now, [CLEARS THROAT] I read Dickens and Victor Hugo, and, also, more modern, Erich Maria Remarque, for example, Hungarian writers. So, the stories that I liked were novels where there was a love story at the center, and then, everybody lived happily ever after. I like that. [LAUGHING] I can't say whether I liked a specific book much more than others, but I just like to read a lot.

INT: Did you learn Russian? Did you ever learn how to read?

AS: Well, yes. We had ten years of Russian, and I was very good in Russian, too. I can barely remember how to read because we did not want to learn Russian. And, there was never any conversational language taught. For example, except for the French school, English was my second language. In high school, we could pick a second language. I picked English, but the teachers had never been in England or any English-speaking country, so they learned from somebody who learned, and I'm sure they didn't speak well, either, but they taught a lot of grammar. So I came here after learning a lot of grammar, barely knowing how to speak, but, you know, having an idea about the language, which was a good thing, after all.

INT: And, did your family and you stay in Bucharest after you finished high school?

AS: YeS, we stayed the whole time. Yes.

INT: Okay. All right. How long, do you recall which year you ended up leaving Bucharest?

AS: Yes, so I left in either late 1960 or '61.

INT: Okay.

AS: I'm not sure of the year, but the way leaving Romania worked, like everything else, it was absurd. [CLEARS THROAT] There was an apartment shortage. So if you had a nice apartment, say, the state wanted the apartment, so they came to visit the apartments, and if you had a nice apartment, you had a better chance to leave. So, what happened with the application process for immigration, We had applied, I don't know when, like I said, my whole life, I waited to leave. But, at some point, they said, "Okay, whoever wants to leave for the 'reunification of the family,'" it was called, "should come to the police and fill out a petition and the forms, and they will be considered as people to leave." In truth, what happened, the Jewish organizations paid for everybody. They basically bought us. And the price depended on the person's age and qualifications. So, at first, it was good business for Romania to send all the old people to Israel. They sent the old people, who now, they didn't have to pay any pensions, and they got rid of old people, no medical, nothing, right? But then, of course, everybody noticed that this wasn't right, so they started sending young people, working age people. [CLEARS THROAT] So, they came to see our apartment. And like I said, we lived in a nice place and had a nice apartment. And at the time, I already knew my husband. We had been friends for several years, and we had become romantically involved. So, when they looked at my apartment, I knew that my family was going to leave pretty soon. So we sat down and we decided that, rather than being separated, we never knew when his family was going to leave. Rather than being separated, in order to have a better chance to leave, we would get married. So we got married, in a hurry. What happened after people deposited these petitions to leave the country, the names were taken down and they immediately lost their jobs. It worked very quickly. And whoever was in college, got kicked out of college, which is what happened to my husband. He was a few months away from his engineering degree. He was already in fifth year, the second half of the fifth year, when he was just working on a project for his degree. So he was kicked out of college. However, I was on maybe on another list, I don't know, but they didn't know about me. So I kept going to college until we got approved to leave. And nobody knew, and we didn't want to tell anybody that we were married, because I didn't want to be associated with a dangerous element like him, officially. And, so, we were married in secret. Except for our families, we didn't tell anybody. We went to the to the city hall to get married. We had a civil marriage. We were very young, twenty-one, twenty-two. And we went there, we didn't know what we needed. You needed to have some medical certificates. We got the medical certificates, we went to the town city hall, and they said, "Where are your witnesses?" We didn't have any witnesses, even. So, we grabbed a couple of people from the waiting room, and, you know, paid them a little something, and they were the witnesses. Never saw them since. And we got married, it was official. Fine. So we kept this secret and hiding from everybody, including our friends, because we didn't want anybody to know. So what happened next?

INT: What year was this, Agnes? What year did you get married?

AS: I got married on January 7th, 1960.

INT: Okay.

AS: Okay. So, the next thing that happened was nothing, in my case, but they came to see his apartment, and his apartment was something else. So right away, somebody very, very important wanted that apartment. And they gave us a guy from the security, state security with a black limousine. And they took us to all the places where we had to file the documents. And, what other people, it took them months to go through this whole bureaucratic process, took us barely enough time, like, two weeks, by the time we woke up. And the irony is that they took your apartment, but you had to leave it immaculate and freshly painted. So that was the other thing that they took it, but you had to paint it. Anyway, we didn't care. We were not allowed to take anything other than a suitcase with personal clothing. No papers, just a suitcase, and then, a trunk, a small trunk with household items, so, like, a duvet, we took dishes,, but it was pretty small, so... And again, no paper, I wrapped everything in towels. Anyway, we were all packed. No jewelry, whatsoever, except the wedding band.

What else? We were not allowed anything, just, no money. We just left. So, the rumors were that if you bought salami, there's a Hungarian salami, this was the rumor. Hungarian salami, and the kind of liqueur, very strong plum liqueur, you could sell those, you know, once you got out of Romania. And, we left based on my husband's approval to leave. And the affidavit for that family came from my mother-in-law's distant cousin in Brazil, which she hadn't hasn't seen in years. The other thing was that when they looked at his apartment and they saw who the people living there were, I was not on the list. So they said, "She can't leave." My mother-in-law was, you know, thinking on her feet, and she said, "We're not leaving without her." And that did it, so I left with them. And the place we went, the first place, we went to Vienna.

So we went to Vienna. And, of course, we couldn't sell the salami, we couldn't sell the the liqueur, So we were left with all this salami we ate. We didn't have money, we ate a lot of salami. I have not touched a salami since then. It's been years. So we got to Vienna, where my mother-in-law was from a different part of Romania and my father-in-law also, they were from Moldova, and my mother-in-law was from Czernowitz. She really considered herself Austrian. This was a part of the country that changed hands. So my mother-in-law, her native language was German, and my father-in-law had studied in Germany, he had studied engineering in Germany, and my mother-in-law studied at the Vienna Conservatory. She was a pianist.

All these-- I'm going a little back in time because I remembered, all these came in very handy when nobody had a job and they were kicked out of college, because, since they all knew how to play an instrument pretty well, my husband was hired to install TV antennas on the roof. It was a new thing in Romania, TVs. So, they were sloped roofs but he was very good. He had spent his vacations in the mountains, so he was good with the ropes-- And, anyway, so he did that. And then, they played with the Romanian Radio Orchestra, so that brought him some income. In fact, they hired him to this roofing antenna company because he could play the violin. And different companies in Romania had different orchestras, and then, there were competitions. So, this is how this worked. So, when we got to Vienna, my mother-in-law had cousins there, and my father-in-law, in Romania, was the head of a... I don't know if I should say the name or not. Of a company, let's say, German-Austrian company, dealing with electrical and electronic things, and he still knew some people. So, he got in touch with them. And, he was immediately hired, my husband was hired, and I was hired. Now, I had never worked before, and I was not finished with my college education, I was in my fourth year. But I knew already, pretty much, in general, and I could always draw very nicely. So I was hired as a draftsperson and they were hired as engineers. The thing with Austria was, they had the Hungarian Revolution in 1956, and all the refugees from Hungary came to Vienna or Austria, and they had enough of refugees, they didn't want any. They made it impossible for people to stay there in Austria, where we wanted to stay, or in Europe, any place. Nobody wanted us, nobody wanted the refugees. Goodbye.

So, the Jewish organization gave us some money and we were supposed to go to Israel. But my in-laws said that they were too old and it would be too hard. Life would be too hard for them in Israel. They didn't know the language, it was very hard, they couldn't work anymore. So then, we had the option of either coming to the United States, going to Canada, or going to Australia. And, as hard as we tried, we could not stay in Austria. So we opted for the United States, and from then on, the IRC, International Rescue Committee was the organization that sponsored us, and we got a small sum of money which added to the little bit that we worked, you know, was enough, sort of, to get by in Vienna. So I had a very interesting year in Vienna. And, I worked with this guy, because I didn't speak any German, they gave me a guy to work with who spoke some French. We got along very well. And, the concerts and the opera, you could go for free, or for little, like the price of a bus ticket, for nothing, practically. So we went and we stood at the opera in the last year and it was the same music, we enjoyed it. And actually, the people who really knew music, and they were there with the score, were the ones who went all the way upstairs. And, so we went to concerts, we did a little bit of traveling. Vienna has beautiful surroundings, hills. We went hiking in the hills. You know, we traveled a little bit as much as we could. And Vienna itself, is a beautiful city and has a lot of history. So this was nice, and we were all set to come to the United States.

But, what happened, my husband was connected, worked actually, at work. He worked with a man who was connected with officials. We don't know exactly how or what. He was connected with different officials, and he worked on our stay in Vienna. So, I was already packing to leave and was still going to work, and he found out that maybe we could stay, there was a chance that we could stay. Meantime, at work, my friend, the French-speaking guy, you know, we've gotten friendly working side by side, and he told me things, personal things as well. And he told me this story, that he had worked for an American outfit, let's say. And they were interrogating-- not interrogating, that's the wrong word. They were interviewing the Hungarians who came out of Hungary to find out where various strategic points, important factories, hydroelectric works were. And he being a draftsman, he and another draftsman, they would draw these things as described and place them on maps. And, so, apparently this thing was winding down. The Hungarian Revolution was in the past, and everybody had left, except for the two bosses, and the two bosses were Jewish. So one day, my guy was talking to his colleague and saying, "Something funny is going on here, and I don't like it. And I hope all these Jews had died in concentration camps."

And he's telling me the story, right? And I'm listening, I always listen to the end of everybody's story. So, he told me this, and his coworker had a different opinion. Immediately, he went to the bosses and told them. And my friend found himself on the street within minutes, check in hand, goodbye. And it was such a shock. I don't know if it was the shock, but he got vertigo, he wasn't able to get out of bed for six weeks. But when this happened, you know, we were hesitating about, "Should we stay? Should we go?" I went back home, not home, wherever we stayed. And I said, "I'm not staying in this country. I don't want my children to grow up in this atmosphere." So we decided to come here.

INT: And how long did you live in Vienna?

AS: A year.

INT: One year.

AS: We had a great time. I mean, we were young, and it didn't matter we didn't have money, so...

INT: Why did you and your husband and in-laws, feel like you wanted to leave Bucharest in the first place, to go to Vienna? What were the reasons?

AS: Well, while under the communist regime, everybody or all my acquaintances and friends and all the Jewish people, I could say, everybody thought of one thing, "How to leave, how to get to the other side of the Iron Curtain, how to get to be free?" So, that's why everybody who could applied to leave. And that was our goal, to leave.

And I know people, you know, I know my friends, the guys discussing how to swim over the Danube, for example. And, I actually met somebody who did that and managed to get to Yugoslavia. The goal was Yugoslavia, which was a relatively freer, semi-communist country at the special situation. So all we wanted when we lived there was to leave. So we left, there was no question. We left everything behind, furniture, carpets, jewelry, everything, sold for nothing, really. My mother-in-law had a Bechstein piano, just sold for nothing. So we left. It didn't matter what we could take with us, it meant nothing. And my father, actually, always said that, "The only thing you really have is up here, in your head." Which is true.

INT: Did your parents and brothers stay behind? Did they continue to live in Bucharest?

AS: My parents were divorced by then, so my brother and my mother left and ended up in Israel. And, my mother had a sister there, and, eventually, they were joined by her brother and his family.

And my brother was a teenager at the time. He lived on a kibbutz, he was a soldier in the Israeli army, he was in two wars. And, so he... you know, he was trying to continue his studies while working, while having a family, while being called in the reserves. So, for all these reasons, he decided to come here, and we sponsored him and he came here and brought his family. His wife is a sabra, and their children, two of their children were born in Israel and one was born here.

INT: And, when you say "here"...

AS: Here in the States.

INT: ...where is here?

AS: Here in the States.

INT: So you were in Vienna for one year?

AS: Yes.

INT: And came to the United States. What year was that?

AS: So, I think it was '61, something like that. Yeah.

INT: And you came with your husband and mother and father-in-law at that time?

AS: Yes. So the IRC or the Joint, or I don't know exactly who gave the money, they put us up first in a hotel. In that time, it was near Columbia University...

INT: In New York?

AS: 112th Street, in New York. And we lived there for a while, quite a while. Then, when we got jobs, we moved all in together, to an apartment in Washington Heights. And then, my husband and I, of course, we moved to this building where I've lived since then, changing apartments, but we lived here since then.

INT: Where do you live now? [INTERPOSING VOICES]

AS: I live in Riverdale, which is right on the Hudson River, my apartment is overlooking the Palisades Parkway, and it's a beautiful view. And I always loved it, never got tired of it.

And I have a huge terrace.

INT: Beautiful.

What were some of your first experiences and reactions as a newly-arrived refugee in the United States?

AS: So my first reaction was that here everything was upside down. Just to give a slight example, in Europe, people go to work, however dressed they are, as long as it's decent enough. But, on Sundays and weekends, they dress up. Here, it's just the reverse.

The food was a surprise, especially the bread which tasted like cotton. So, that wasn't a good surprise. The supermarkets were amazing. You know, the abundance of food. Already in Vienna, we had a little bit of a taste of good food, of course, but here it was another thing, so, something to get used to. We did not have a lot of money in the beginning at all, so we had enough money to eat, but not enough money to take the subway, sometimes. So we walked a lot, anyway, we liked to walk. And the first thing when we landed in this hotel, it was near Broadway, so we went out to Broadway, and we said, "Okay, let's explore this street, Broadway, let's walk to the end of it." So this was 112th Street, and we kept walking and walking until we got, I don't know, if we got to around 60th Street or something like that. We said, "That's a long street." [PAUSES] Yes. [DRINKING]

INT: Were you and your husband able to find work when you got here?

AS: I found work first.

And I was fired from my first two jobs. Actually fired, not laid off, fired, and I'll tell you how. And then, he found work. He was not fired. [LAUGHING] So, the first job, my first job was provided by, I think, it was Joint, they had an agency, the Jewish organization had an agency to provide jobs. And they told me this is a temporary job, but I didn't know, "temporary," what is that even. So, I took this job, and the owner was actually, he spoke Hungarian. It was a very small company. I didn't know, small company, big company, didn't know anything. They were maybe, I don't know, four people and a secretary.

So, I worked and they gave me a drawing to do. And I didn't know English very well, so I didn't want to ask him any questions. And I didn't even understand completely what I was told. I usually said, "yes," and then, watched the reaction of the other person and changed it to "no" sometimes. So I worked there for two weeks and I produced a drawing, almost finished, and now I laugh about the drawing. It was a site plan with piping and manholes and whatever, but I didn't know what was what, so I showed all the benches, they were there, I drew them in the side, which wasn't important at all, I left out the manholes. Anyway, but it was a nice-looking drawing and I stepped away from my desk. The secretary came at that moment, and when I came back, she said, "I am so sorry, I fell on your drawing and I ripped it." So, here was two weeks worth of work, and the secretary, who had been there a long time, seemed to be on friendly terms with the boss. "Am I going to go and complain about her? Am I going to say anything?" So, while I was debating this dilemma, the boss called me in, and he said, "I am sorry this is not working out. You're too slow." Now, I had to start the whole thing all over, and he said, "You're too slow" because there's nothing to be seen from the two weeks. So, I found out later, when I ran into other immigrants who had all passed through this company, and they said that the guy hired immigrants to have a tax deduction. So that's why it didn't last very long.

INT: Yeah.

AS: So then, I had a better job. [PAUSES] A better job with another engineering company, and I started to feel quite at home there, and I was doing very well. Interestingly enough, the person I ended up working with was German. A very nice man, he was like a father to me. He had, I don't know, how many daughters, he practically adopted me. And he liked me for having all the good German attributes. And, you know, again, we talked and he said, my name is not whatever his name was, but I changed my name when I came here. Okay. Later, he told me that he had had a factory, an ammunition factory, in Germany, and he employed a lot of concentration camp prisoners. But the guy saw himself like another Schindler. Except that whatever he produced, as opposed to Schindler's factory, was good to use, you know, so... But he had such a different point of view. You know, when it came to the war, in his mind, this was unavoidable for Germany because they got such a bad deal after World War I that they had to do this, the economy was bad, this, that. It was a necessity in his mind, and it was so interesting. I have to add that my father never hated the Germans. He felt that this could happen to ordinary people, and we actually saw it happening during communism, too. You had to keep a low profile. You had to do what you were told to do. You had to go to meetings. You had to participate. To go to the university, you had to join the youth organization. I could not have gone to the university without lying about my background, because the way it was, it was free. Sixty percent workers, thirty percent peasants, and ten percent middle class, that's how the things were. And I was lucky, I had been a good student in high school, that I got into college without an exam, and we lied when we filled out the papers, and we said that my father was always an employee. They didn't have what we have now, databases. So, yeah, so that was interesting. Anyway, to get back to that job, the man taught me a lot of things. I really owe him a lot. And I learned pretty good English while I was working there. Now, what happened in this company, the original owners were dead. They were very good engineers, three of them. And the sons who inherited the company, some were nicer, some were smarter. The principal of the company happened to be neither nice nor smart. Didn't do anything all day, sat with his feet on the desk and read the New York Times. And, he had an organizational chart on the wall showing himself at the top. It so happens that another Romanian girl started working there, and we became very good friends. We happened to live nearby, and she had a tragic story. Her husband had died very young, very young. She came with her in-laws. Anyway, we were friends and we were talking one day. Officially, we had a lunch hour of half an hour and we had two coffee breaks, one in the morning, one in the afternoon. Officially ten minutes, okay. So, during those ten minutes, she came to talk to me and she was telling me that she had another job offer, but she didn't feel like leaving because this was, you know, a nice place to work for, and I was there, and she felt very comfortable here. And the principal of the company came, walked by, this was around Christmas. He walked by, he must have been drunk, too, because he said, you know, "The break is over." My friend didn't say anything. I said, you know, knowing English but not enough English to know the difference between the subtleties of the language. I said, "Oh yeah," and because of this, "Oh, yeah," which I meant to say, "I am sorry, I didn't know," but it came out as, "Oh, yeah?" he went to the chief of personnel and we both got fired right away. So, everybody was very sorry to see us go. There was really no reason. And, well, my friend, of course, accepted the job that she was offered. I got a better pay job, which is sometimes what happens when you get fired or laid off. And, Then, the jobs I had, where I worked for nice places, one of the companies, I worked for 23 years.

AS: So, yeah.

INT: What was your profession there?

AS: I was a mechanical designer. So, in the beginning, we did manual drafting and design calculations. But then, when everything got computerized, I learned AutoCAD very well and we worked on AutoCAD and the design part also. We had different programs to design. I worked on air conditioning systems. And, the company that I worked for, a long time, 23 years, did a lot of international work, too. They did a lot of airports, I worked on a lot of airports, and hydroelectric central in Pakistan. Very interesting projects. And the people were very nice, and it was conveniently located close to Grand Central on 42nd Street.

INT: Made your commute easier.

AS: Made my commute easier, yes.

INT: And when you were in New York, did you and your husband, did you have any children during this time? When you arrived and were working?

AS: So the first ten years, we didn't have children. And, you know, being out, it was just such a relief. And we were so eager to see Europe, to travel, to go, and we did. We were working, we took five months off, everybody said we were crazy. And all the money that we had saved, we spent it on traveling for five months. We had an open airline ticket, so you could go from one place to another, it was included in the price of this ticket. And, you know, it was much cheaper in those days, we stayed in cheap places. And, you know, we ate in cheap places, we had a wonderful time. And, you know, we saw...

We didn't go to the northern countries because we liked the warm weather. But we went to England, Holland, France, Spain, Portugal, Greece, Switzerland, Italy and Israel.

INT: And what year was this, Agnes?

AS: This was before my children were born, so my first child was born in 1970. So, this was actually 1968. I remember the year because we almost went to Prague, but then, there was the Prague Spring, so we decided not to take any chances.

INT: That sounds like a lovely trip, to say the least.

AS: It was a lovely trip, I am not sorry that we spent the money on this.

INT: [LAUGHING] And so, you had your first child in 1970?

AS: Yes. So, both my children were born here, 1970 and, very fast, one after the other.

INT: What are their names?

AS: So my oldest daughter is Nadia Leigh, this Leigh is her middle name. And my youngest daughter is Jill, and her middle name is Danielle.

INT: And, where do they live now? My youngest daughter, Jill, lives in Los Angeles. After college, she spent two years in Prague, ironically, just where we didn't go, teaching English. And I had to drag her back from there because, you know, I was afraid she-- All my life, I tried to get out of Eastern Europe, and she goes back now. But, eventually, she came back and she settled in L.A. And my oldest daughter, who used to live in Brooklyn and loved it, got married and moved to Stamford, Connecticut, which isn't too far.

INT: Yeah. How did you choose your daughters' names?

AS: So both my children were premature and I was unprepared, but Nadia was suggested by my mother, and Leigh was a version of "Leah," which is the Jewish name. And after Nadia, and everybody in the park asking me, "Nadia, Nadia, how do you pronounce it?" I said, "Enough with the complicated names." So I named Jill, "Jill," and nobody had any questions about that. And, that was it.

INT: When they were younger, what language or languages did you speak to them growing up?

AS: We started speaking Romanian in the house, my husband and I spoke Romanian. And, eventually, they went to school and they learned English. I didn't want them to learn English from me and get my accent, God forbid. So, they went to school, they learned English, and then, they started answering in English, and, you know, by answering in English, we ended up by talking English all the time.

INT: Yeah. Have you ever talk to them about your experiences surviving the Holocaust, surviving the war?

INT: Have you shared much with them?

AS: Yes. I shared a lot with them.

AS: So everything I told you, they pretty much knew at a fairly young age. I am not sure, but my daughter, Jill, she had to interview somebody for her school work. She was in grammar school. She had to interview somebody. And, of course, I was right here, so she interviewed me. And when I told the story about the war, she took this to school, and the teacher cried. She came back telling me, the teacher cried. So, they knew very early. And my grandchildren, my daughter Jill has two sons. My grandchildren also know. And, actually, one of them was very interested in taking a video, and we started, but we never finished. You know, we didn't have enough time. And then, the part that he did somehow got lost. So, this will be very good for them, all in one piece.

INT: What are your grandchildren's names?

AS: Liam is the oldest one, and Dylan is the youngest son. So it's Liam Jonah and Dylan Noah.

INT: And how old are they?

AS: The oldest one is 14, and the youngest one is 12.

INT: Okay. And, their dad, did he grow up in the United States?

AS: No. Their dad is British.

INT: Oh, British.

AS: So their last name is Hardy.

INT: Have they been able to travel to the U.K.?

AS: Yes. They go, yes, quite often with their father for extended periods of time, vacations.

INT: And what-- So it sounds like they're interested in hearing about your story, they know about it.

AS: They're very interested in history.

INT: Yeah.

AS: And World War II and World War I, especially that time period, I think, it also comes from seeing movies.

INT: Are there things that you would like to share with them or kids of their generation in this moment, in our present day, given everything you have lived through and survived?

AS: You know, it's very difficult for me to come up with, you know, some kind of a wise, wise sentence. But I would say that, you know, it's a problem when we see the world as "us" and "them" and you have to have an open mind. There is no us and them, really. We're all pretty much the same. When you travel, you realize how most average people have the same aspirations, and most of them are good but can do very bad things. And there is a danger in this polarization.

INT: What would you say now, in our present moment of the state of Israel and the conflict, for example, that's happening? How do you talk about that with your grandchildren or what do you feel about what's happening right now in the Middle East?

AS: So I have to say, without any doubt, Israel has the right to exist and has the right to defend itself. And it's very important for us, it was important to know that there was a place we could go to and be welcome, and where nobody is anti-Semitic. It made all the difference in the world. We lived with the hope that someday we'll get out of Romania and go someplace where you can be free to be Jewish. And I thought that, here in the United States, would be like this. And I thought that there were no, or at least not many anti-Semites. So, this time period for me is very difficult. It's traumatic, and it brings back very bad memories. And having said this, you know, I don't think all wars, when you think back, all wars are so useless. You think between England and France, there was a 100-year war. What happened after World War II? Former enemies are friends and allies. What was the whole thing about, right?

INT: Would you say that surviving what you have throughout your life has impacted your faith, your religion, your identity in any way?

AS: Definitely, somebody's experience is an important part in forming their personality. And, so, it is undeniable. But what's also important is, how you react to something. So the same experience might have a different impact on different people. So, obviously, whatever my life was, you know, very important and left a mark. All the good things and the bad things.

But the living through hard times makes you stronger.

You don't feel entitled to anything.

You feel you have to work for everything.

INT: Have your experiences affected how you raised your daughters or what you impart on your grandchildren?

AS: So whatever I learned from my parents, as I said, from my mother, to be open, honest, hardworking, thorough. And, from my father,

I tried to impart to my children. And what I learned from my father, actually, was not to be a pessimist. And, you know, to be independent, to manage in the world the way he managed in Romania, you really had to know how to work the system and everything.

I learned to be independent, and I learned to rely on my abilities to survive, to support myself. I never thought that I would be a housewife forever. I was home for seven and a half years after my children were born, but I had no intention to not go back to work.

INT: How would you identify yourself now?

AS: I would identify myself as American, as culturally Jewish, let's say. After the war, when we didn't have much to eat, we gave up the idea of being kosher, obviously. And then, we kind of lost it. And, so, we celebrate the holidays, the major holidays with family. My brother lives in New Jersey. We go out there or they come here. And my children were brought up in this spirit. So, my children are not religious, either.

I taught them not to be prejudiced. So, they're not. And I, you know, I taught them to be what's called a mensch. But I don't know if you apply the term to girls, too, I guess. [UNCLEAR] So, right. Yeah, you can say that.

INT: Have you ever returned back to your hometown, the country where you grew up, what is now the country that you grew up?

AS: Never. Never. I never returned, my brother never returned. We didn't.

INT: Yeah. Any interest?

AS: Chapter closed. Chapter closed. The girls wanted to go back. At some point, I was thinking, maybe. But then we never made it so.

INT: Yeah.

Just a few more questions for you before we close. Why did you feel so strongly to come forward and share your story with the USC Shoah Foundation?

AS: That's a good question. I think it's natural, when one gets older, to, sort of, look back and wonder about the good things that you did, the mistakes that you made, your contribution to others, to society. So, without being like Einstein or something, all of us contribute to our immediate-- the people who are surrounding us, right. And, I thought that this was a very important thing for me to do, to tell my story, to let everybody know, that, yes, the Holocaust happened. I lived through it, I lost my family. I saw terrible things afterwards, families destroyed. And most of the survivors went on to build new lives and have new families. And I think, nowadays, when there is so much anti-Semitism, just to see that the Jews are not that different from everybody else is helpful.

And, that's about it. I think it was important for me to do this.

INT: And we thank you for sharing your story. Is there anything else you would like to tell your grandchildren, future generations, given everything you've lived through in your life?

AS: Well, I think everything I said is what I wanted to say. I don't think I left anything important out. And thank you very much for giving me this opportunity.

INT: Thank you, Agnes. I don't have anything else for right now.

